-l @gz
-t @gz
# `-n`, and a hash rather than raw bytes, are both load-bearing.
#
# gzip stores the input file's MTIME in the header (RFC 1952 section 2.3.1,
# bytes 4-7), and this harness materialises the fixtures in TWO scratch trees,
# one per side. Their mtimes need not fall in the same second, so a bare
# `-c @f` compares the clock, not the tool: measured 2026-11-06, the same
# binary on the same content produced `1f8b 0808 eecd ed6a` and
# `1f8b 0808 e09c 5569` across a touch, while system and bundle gzip agreed
# byte for byte at a fixed mtime. `-n` suppresses the name and timestamp.
#
# The hash keeps the comparison honest for a second reason: the harness
# captures output through a command substitution, which drops null bytes, so
# raw DEFLATE output is compared lossily and bash warns about it.
-nc @f | shasum -a 256
